## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) represent a large category of 3-dimensional (3D) porous materials by linkage of inorganic transition metal clusters and organic ligands [1–3], featuring unlimited structure versatility, high surface area, tunable functions, facile synthesis and good stability, and thus have been demonstrated for gas adsorption [4], selective separation [5], catalysis [6], drug delivery [7], sensing [8], and energy applications [9–10]. Among them, the potentials of developing MOF-based energy storage materials such as lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) have been driving substantial research interest recently [11]. Due to their uniform structures, high surface area and tunable porosities [12], MOFs can serve as excellent precursors for synthesis of porous transition metal oxides or metal oxides embedded in porous carbon matrices [13]. Several MOF structures, such as MIL-88-Fe [14], MOF-5 [15], ZIF-8 (Zn, Co) [16], have been demonstrated for the synthesis of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , $Z_{ Ḋ }\mathrm{nO@CḌ}$ , $\mathrm{Zn}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{3-x}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanostructures, respectively. Nonetheless, metal oxides resulted from pyrolysis of MOFs often lose their original 3D, highly ordered frameworks, reducing their structural uniqueness and potentials as functional porous materials.
The direct fabrication of MOF-based LIB electrodes has also been investigated recently as an alternative approach for energy storage. The metal cations in the MOF structures serve as active sites for redox reactions [17], and the open crystal frameworks provide efficient and reversible insertion and extraction of ions in the either aqueous or organic electrolytes [18]. For instance, Prussian blue and its analogs [19–21], MOF-177 [22], MIL-53(Fe) [23],
$Z_{\mathrm{{n}_{3}}}(\mathrm{{HCOO})_{6}}$ [24], and Mn–LCP [25] have been demonstrated as electrode materials for LIBs. However, the structure variations during lithiation and delithiation cycles can result in the pulverization of the 3D rigid frameworks of MOFs. In addition, the organic ligands of MOFs are susceptible to the organic electrolytes especially in the charge/discharge conditions. Thus, the highly ordered MOF structures are often lost after the electrochemical tests, accompanying by the loss of clear X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns.
In order to explore the potential of MOFs as LIB electrodes with high electrochemical stability, herein, we developed a flexible, 2D MOF structure using Ni ions as nodes and $_{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetramethyl$^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyrazole $\mathrm{(H_{2}M e_{4}b p z)}$ as the organic ligands. $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Me}_{4}\mathrm{bpz}$ has been mostly studied in a protonated form, such as hydrogen-bonding synthon [26] or neutral bidentate [27], and has also been used for the synthesis of ${\sf A g}^{+}$ and ${\mathsf{C u}}^{+}.$ -coordinating MOFs [28–29]. Previous studies have shown that MOFs with azolate-based ligands have higher chemical and thermal stability than those with carboxylate and/or pyridyl ligands [30], thus enabling higher stability under electrochemical conditions. More importantly, the two pyrazolate (pz) rings of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Me}_{4}\mathrm{bpz}$ can rotate around the central C–C single bond [29], which offers the MOF structure much flexibility to adjust the framework variation during the intercalation and deintercalation of Li-ions (Scheme 1). This structure flexibility resembles the ‘‘breathing effect’’ observed in several MOFs such as MIL53 during gas absorption [31]. Moreover, the binding of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Me}_{4}\mathrm{bpz}$ ligands to Ni ions forms a 2D, layered MOF architecture $(\mathsf{N i-M e}_{4}.$ - bpz), which allows for more flexibility than 3D structures for efficient insertion/extraction of Li-ions. As a proof-of-concept, LIB anodes directly made of the Ni–Me4bpz MOF structure shows good storage capacity and cycling performance, with structure well maintained after the electrochemical measurements, suggesting the potential of utilizing this flexible, layered nickel-based MOFs for energy storage.
Finally, these Ni–Me4bpz MOFs can also been thermally converted into nickel oxides, with their capability as LIB anodes being investigated.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
2,4-pentanedione was purchased from J&K Chemical Company. DEF (Diethylformamide) was purchased from Aladdin. NaOH, $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ diethyl ether, hydrazine $(\mathsf{N}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{4})$ , methanol, and ${\mathsf{N i C l}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ were
purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Company. All chemicals were commercial products of reagent grade, and were used without further purification.
## 2.2. Synthesis of the organic ligand H2Me4bpz
The ligand was synthesized according to a previous literature [32], as shown in Scheme 2. Briefly, $200{\mathrm{g}}$ of 2,4-Pentanedione and $48\ \mathrm{g}$ of NaOH was added to $1500\mathrm{ml}$ of $\mathrm{Et}_{2}0$ under the protection of nitrogen and continuously stirring. Then used the Soxhlet extractor containing $254{\mathrm{g}}$ of $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ and $2\mathrm{~L~}$ of $\mathrm{Et}_{2}0$ to extract for $^{6\mathrm{{h}}}$ to get the mediate. Put $6\mathrm{ml}$ of ${\sf N}_{2}{\sf H}_{4}$ to $5\mathrm{ml}$ of water containing $4.96{\mathrm{g}}$ of mediate and stirred for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . Finally, the resultant was recrystallized by methanol and evaporated to get the white powder of $\mathrm{H_{2}M e_{4}b p z}$ .
