## 2.5. Electrochemical measurement
The LIB cells were assembled using coin-type half cells (2016 type) with Li metal as counter electrode. $1\mathrm{MLiPF_{6}}$ in ethylene carbonate (EC)/diethylene carbonate (DEC) $(1{:}1~\mathrm{w}/\mathrm{w})$ was used as electrolyte, and Celgard 2400 membrane was employed as separator. Electrodes were prepared by mixing active materials $\left(\mathsf{N i-M e}_{4}.\right.$ bpz or NiO, $80\mathrm{wt\%}$ ), super $\mathrm{~\bf~P~}$ carbon black $(10\mathrm{wt\%})$ , and polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF, $10\mathrm{\:wt\%}$ ) in N-methyl-2-pyrrolidinone solvent to form a homogeneous slurry. The slurry was spread onto a copper foil. Coated electrodes were dried in a vacuum oven at $80^{\circ}C$ overnight and then pressed. The electrochemical tests were tested using a multichannel battery workstation (Neware Co. China).
## 3. Results and discussions
The crystal structure of the $\mathrm{Ni-Me_{4}b p z}$ sample was characterized by single X-ray diffraction. As shown in Fig. 1a, the Ni(II) ion is six-coordinated with four N atoms from four deprotonated $\mathsf{M e}_{4}.$ bpz ligands, and two Cl atoms from nickel chloride. The bond lengths of Ni1–N2, Ni1–N3 and Ni1–Cl2 are 2.093, 2.160, and $2.472\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ , respectively, indicating that the Ni(II) ion is in a distorted octahedron geometry. Besides, the ligands are two-coordinated, in which the two coordinated N atoms are in the diagonal positions, and the two pyrazolate (pz) rings of $\mathrm{H_{2}M e_{4}b p z}$ rotate with a dihedral angle of $84.9^{\circ}$ , which is different from the crystals $\begin{array}{r l}{\lefteqn{\big[\mathrm{Ag}_{2}\big(\mathrm{Me}_{4}.}}\end{array}$ bpz)] reported previously $(63.3^{\circ},~67.4^{\circ})$ [29]. The rotation of adjacent pyrazolate rings provides a lower energy state, which is flexible for variations of the framework. Each ligand bridges two Ni(II) ions to form an infinite 2D layer network. As shown in Fig. 1b, repeated quadrilateral units are observed, and each unit consists of four ligands and four Ni(II) ions, which forms a closed loop containing 32 atoms. When the Ni(II) ion and the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Me}_{4}\mathrm{bpz}$ ligands are regarded as vertexes and edges, a sql topology is obtained (Fig. 1c).
Before taking the thermogravimetric analysis (TGA), the framework was dried at $80^{\circ}C$ in vacuum in order to remove the solvent in the structure. The TGA results for $\mathrm{Ni-Me_{4}b p z}$ in air shows that the weight loss starts at about $350^{\circ}\mathsf C$ (Fig. S2), suggesting that the framework is thermally stable below $350^{\circ}\mathsf C$ The weight loss continues until about $500^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ , due to the decomposition of the organic ligands. The weight of the final nickel oxide is about $15.4\%$ of the original Ni–Me4bpz sample, consistent with the formula percentage $(14.8\%)$ . Nitrogen sorption isotherm of the Ni– $\mathtt{M e}_{4}\mathtt{b p z}$ at 77 K reveals that the crystal has a BET surface area of $67\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Fig. S3).
The electrochemical performance of Ni–Me4bpz for LIB anodes was first investigated by cyclic voltammetry (CV) in the range of $0.01{-}3.0\mathrm{V}$ at a scan rate of $0.05\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ (Fig. 3a). In the first cycle of the CV curves, three cathodic peaks are observed. The cathodic peaks at around 1.25 and $0.75\mathrm{V}$ corresponded to the initial formation of $[\mathrm{Li}_{2}{-}\mathrm{Me}_{4}\mathrm{bp}z]$ and nickel metal, as well as the alloying process during the first discharge [24], and the peak at around $0.6\ensuremath{\mathrm{V}}$ corresponded to the formation of amorphous $\mathrm{Li}_{2}0$ and a solid-electrolyte interphase (SEI) layer [33]. In addition, the oxidation peak at $1.15\mathrm{V}$ corresponds to the reformation of $\mathrm{Ni-Me_{4}b p z}$ [25]. The three cathodic peaks disappeared after the first cycle, leaving an initial capacity loss.
The $\mathrm{Ni-Me}_{4}\mathrm{bpz}$ electrode was further investigated by galvanostatic charge/discharge cycling tests, with several representative charge/discharge cycles displayed (Fig. 3b). The first discharge specific capacity is $320\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and the second discharge specific capacity drops to $140\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{hg}^{-1}$ , corresponding to a capacity loss about $56.25\%$ . This capacity loss arises mainly from the incomplete conversion reaction and irreversible lithium loss due to the formation of the SEI layer [34], similar to the CV curves. The specific capacity remains stable from the third cycle, and is retained at about $120\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles, and the Coulombic efficiency remains above $98\%$ (Fig. 3c), indicating excellent cycling stability.
In addition, powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of the Ni– Me4bpz electrode measured before and after the electrochemical tests show clear diffraction peaks. Although the X-ray diffractions are broadened after the electrochemical tests due to the small variations of framework structures during the Li-ion intercalation and de-intercalation process in the electrochemical tests, but the position of peaks still fits well with the simulated results (Fig. 3d), indicating the crystal structure of the $\mathrm{Ni-Me_{4}b p z}$ electrodes is well preserved after the LIB tests. These results confirm that the 2D layered structure and flexible ligands can facilitate the Li-ion insertion/extraction and the electrochemical stability of Ni–Me4bpz.
Finally, the capability of utilizing $\mathsf{N i-M e_{4}b p z}$ as a sacrificial precursor for synthesis of NiO is also demonstrated. A two-step calcination method is used to pyrolysis of $\mathrm{Ni-Me}_{4}\mathrm{bpz}$ into NiO (Experimental section). The as-synthesized Ni–Me4bpz was first heated to $500^{\circ}\mathsf C$ under ${\sf N}_{2}$ atmosphere, as the carbon generated during this process can act as a buffer layer to preserve the framework and decrease the structure contraction. Afterwards, the sample was calcined in air to obtain NiO. The PXRD pattern of the calcined sample display two distinct peaks at $37.33^{\circ}$ and $43.42^{\circ}$ (2h), corresponding to the (111) and (200) diffractions of NiO (JCPDS card No. 47–1049). No other peaks are observed, suggesting the high purity of NiO after the thermal treatment. The CV curves of the obtained NiO product show a pair of redox peaks at around 0.9 and $2.3\mathrm{V}$ in the first three cycles, corresponding to the reduction and oxidation processes of NiO [33]. The representative discharge/charge voltage profiles and the summarized results of the as-synthesized NiO are exhibited, in a voltage window of 0.01– $3.0\mathrm{V}$ at $50\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ (Fig. 4c and d). The specific capacity of the first cycle is about $1200\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which drops to $700\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at the second cycle, in accord with the CV curves. The Coulombic efficiency remains above $97\%$ since the 10th cycle, and the specific capacity is retained at about $200\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles. The low specific capacity may be attributed to the poor microstructure of the crystal, as the NiO particles are aggregated after calcination (Fig. 4a, inset), resulting in a lower contact area and poor reactivity with Li-ions.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, we have developed a 2-dimensional, wavy layered metal–organic framework for direct electrochemical energy storage. The Ni-based crystal framework provides large surface area and abundant pores for $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ intercalation, and the $\mathtt{M e}_{4}\mathtt{b p z}$ ligands linked to $\mathsf{N i}(\mathrm{II})$ ions allow for excellent structural flexibility and stability. Anodes made of Ni–Me4bpz exhibit an initial specific capacity of $320\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $50\mathrm{mAg^{-1}}$ , and retain at $120\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles. In addition, the $\mathrm{Ni-Me_{4}b p z}$ structure after electrochemical tests still preserves clear XRD patterns, suggesting the effect of the wavy layer and ligand flexibility. Moreover, the Ni–Me4bpz samples can be further calcined into NiO structures, which can also function for Li-ion storage with a reversible specific capacity of $200\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Our results suggest new research opportunities of developing flexible layered MOF structures for efficient energy storage and electrochemical reactions.
